WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
CSRF protection should be tested as part of normal application quality.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:18.280
Reviewers should identify state-changing routes, confirm that framework middleware or equivalent protection applies, and verify that missing, invalid, or expired tokens are rejected before action.

3
00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:26.460
The inventory should include forms, admin actions, settings pages, JavaScript routes, and session-bound API endpoints.

4
00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:30.500
Testing should focus on safe defensive cases.

5
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.680
The goal is not to build attack pages or teach abuse.

6
00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:47.660
The goal is to confirm that protected routes require the expected token or equivalent control, that the failure path does not perform the action, and that newly added custom routes do not bypass the established framework protection.

7
00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:58.500
Logging should record rejected CSRF validation events at a useful level without exposing raw token values, secrets, sensitive request bodies, or unnecessary personal data.

8
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:05.720
A log entry can identify the route, high-level failure reason, timestamp, and safe request context.

9
00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:11.180
It should help defenders investigate misconfiguration without creating a sensitive data leak.

10
00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:16.900
Monitoring can identify unusual rejection patterns, misconfiguration, or regressions.

11
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:25.080
A sudden spike in token failures may indicate a broken frontend release, expired session behavior, proxy issue, or suspicious activity.

12
00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:35.740
Regression tests are especially valuable because CSRF gaps often appear when a team adds a custom form, admin route, or API endpoint outside the standard path.
